 "notes": [
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>A thin coat crisps the pork in the pan and catches the glaze, which it also thickens.</li><li>Potato starch works the same.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Why cornstarch"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Shabu-shabu-thin pork belly (about <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"len\" data-u=\"mm\" data-a=\"2\" data-s=\"0\">2 mm</span>), the longer the better — it wraps without tearing and crisps instead of chewing.</li><li>Thicker slices won't stay closed.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Thin, long slices"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>They're best hot.</li><li>Reheating makes the pork tough and the rice hard; in a bento, keep them cold with an ice pack and eat the same day.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Eat them straight away"
  },
  {
   "html": "<p>Japanese Cooking 101 makes them with paper-thin beef, no starch, a little salt in the rice and a milder sauce (<span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"tbsp\" data-a=\"1.5\" data-s=\"0\">1½ tbsp</span> soy, <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"tbsp\" data-a=\"1\" data-s=\"0\">1 tbsp</span> each of sake, mirin and water, <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"tsp\" data-a=\"1\" data-s=\"0\">1 tsp</span> sugar for 4–5 balls).</p>",
   "title_html": "With beef"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Press a <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"len\" data-u=\"cm\" data-a=\"1\" data-s=\"0\">1 cm</span> cube of cheese — cheddar or mozzarella — into the middle of each barrel before wrapping: it melts while they fry.</li><li>A popular version in Japan, not in the source recipe.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Fun inside (this site's)"
  },
  {
   "html": "<p>Seam side down first, the frying and glazing times, the pork weight and the two-per-person portion are this site's starting points; the method and every amount are Sudachi's.</p>",
   "title_html": "Assumed: what's added to the source"
  }
 ],
